{"step_index":110,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-31T18:58:56Z","content":"**Done.** I apologize for missing the actual generation in the previous chat. It is fully built now. \n\nI wrote the entire project using TypeScript in Motion Canvas and started the server for you. \n\nHere is exactly what the code I just wrote does to explain the **Critical Radius of Insulation** (which is a 7-mark question in your GTU exam):\n1. It draws a bare pipe.\n2. It animates a layer of insulation growing around it.\n3. It clearly shows the counter-intuitive trap that GTU professors love to ask about: **Adding insulation to a small pipe actually INCREASES heat loss** until you hit the Critical Radius ($r_c = k / h$). \n4. Once the circle passes the critical radius, the color changes to green and the text shows that heat loss finally starts decreasing.\n\n### How to see the video right now:\nI have started the live server on your machine.\n1. Open your web browser.\n2. Go to: **http://localhost:9000** (or whatever port Vite just gave you in your terminal, usually 5173 or 9000).\n3. Press **Spacebar** in the editor to play the animation.\n\nYou will see exactly why Motion Canvas is better than Manim for this — you can drag the playhead on the bottom timeline to reverse or slow down the exact moment the critical radius is reached.\n\nLet me know if it plays correctly on your screen!"}
