{"step_index":121,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-31T19:01:41Z","content":"For making YouTube explainer videos (like the Heat Transfer animation I just built for you), **Motion Canvas is 100x better.**\n\nHere is exactly why:\n\n### 1. The Timeline (The Dealbreaker)\n**Motion Canvas** has a built-in visual timeline editor in the browser. If you want the circle to grow exactly when you say the word \"Radius\" in your voiceover, you just drag your voiceover into the browser, look at the audio waveform, and drag the code block to match it. \n**Remotion** does NOT have this. You have to blindly guess the exact frame numbers in your code (`startFrame={120}`) and constantly refresh to see if it lined up with your voice. It is extremely frustrating for storytelling.\n\n### 2. Built for Different Things\n*   **Motion Canvas** was built *by* a YouTuber (aarthificial) specifically for human beings to make educational YouTube videos. It focuses on procedural drawing, math, and teaching.\n*   **Remotion** was built for software engineers to generate *automated* videos at scale. \n\n### When is Remotion better?\nRemotion wins only if you are trying to build an **automated YouTube bot**. \nFor example: If you want to pull live cricket scores from an API and automatically generate and upload a recap video every single night while you are sleeping, Remotion is better because it runs perfectly on a server without a human looking at a timeline.\n\n**Verdict:** \nIf **you** are the one sitting at the computer trying to explain an engineering concept to your audience, stick to **Motion Canvas**. \n\nDid the local server open up properly for you, or do you want me to explain any part of the code I wrote for the critical radius?","thinking":"**Comparing the options**\n\nI'm focusing on a direct comparison between \"Removion\" and \"Motion Canvas\" specifically for YouTube explainer videos, aiming for an unbiased analysis. No tools have been brought in to play. I'm aiming for directness and honesty in the assessment, providing a non-sycophantic comparison.\n\n\n"}
