{"step_index":128,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-31T19:08:14Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-08-31T19:08:14Z sender=9f42ecc5-bab1-4bbc-b5d8-4d3c58536b09 priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=I have completed the research on free and open-source tools for making stickman animations and sketching-style (whiteboard) videos for YouTube. Below is a detailed breakdown of the specific tools you mentioned, along with their pros/cons and a final recommendation on which are easiest for a beginner.\n\n### 1. Excalidraw (with Excalidraw-Animate / Excalimate)\nExcalidraw is an open-source, web-based virtual whiteboard that inherently features a hand-drawn, \"sketchy\" aesthetic. Using plugins like `excalidraw-animate` or screen-recording its live collaboration/drawing features allows you to make excellent whiteboard explainer videos.\n*   **Best For:** Whiteboard-style explainer videos, technical diagrams, and literal sketching animations.\n*   **Pros:**\n    *   **Extremely Beginner-Friendly:** Virtually no learning curve. If you can draw basic shapes or use a mouse, you can use it.\n    *   **Built-in Sketch Aesthetic:** Everything you draw or type automatically looks like a hand-drawn whiteboard sketch.\n    *   **Fast Workflow:** You can put together an explainer video in minutes.\n*   **Cons:**\n    *   Not a traditional animation tool (no timelines, rigging, or frame-by-frame character animation).\n    *   Very limited for complex stickman action sequences.\n*   **Difficulty:** Very Low (Easiest)\n\n### 2. Krita\nKrita is a highly respected open-source digital painting application that includes a dedicated animation workspace.\n*   **Best For:** Traditional, frame-by-frame hand-drawn animations and painterly sketch videos.\n*   **Pros:**\n    *   **Incredible Brushes:** Offers the best drawing and sketching brushes among free tools, perfect for the \"hand-drawn\" look.\n    *   **Intuitive UI for Artists:** If you've used Photoshop or any drawing soft\n<truncated 1774 bytes>\n*\n    *   **Intimidating Interface:** It is a complex 3D software suite. The interface is famously difficult for absolute beginners to grasp just for a simple 2D sketch video.\n    *   Overkill for simple whiteboard presentations.\n*   **Difficulty:** High (Steepest learning curve)\n\n### 5. OpenToonz\nUsed by Studio Ghibli, OpenToonz is a professional, open-source 2D animation software.\n*   **Best For:** Professional-grade 2D character animation and traditional production pipelines.\n*   **Pros:**\n    *   Includes a Skeleton Tool specifically designed for character rigging.\n    *   Powerful scanning features if you want to draw stickmen on physical paper and animate them digitally.\n*   **Cons:**\n    *   Designed for studio environments, meaning the workflow is quite complex and rigid for a solo beginner.\n    *   Frequent reports of crashes or bugs on certain operating systems.\n*   **Difficulty:** High\n\n---\n\n### Additional Beginner Recommendations for Stickmen:\nWhile you didn't explicitly list them, **Pivot Animator** (Windows) and **Stykz** (Mac/Windows) are the undisputed kings of *beginner* stickman animation. They are free (Stykz is open-source-esque, Pivot is freeware) and use simple node-based skeletons. You just drag joints frame-by-frame to make classic stick figure videos. \n\n### Final Conclusion: What is the easiest tool for a beginner?\n\n1. **For Whiteboard/Sketching Explainer Videos:** Use **Excalidraw**. It requires absolutely zero animation knowledge. You simply draw your diagrams and record your screen (or use an animate plugin), and you immediately achieve that YouTube whiteboard aesthetic.\n2. **For Hand-Drawn Frame-by-Frame Stickmen:** Use **Krita**. It's the most natural-feeling drawing software.\n3. **For Smoothly Animated (Rigged) Stickmen:** If you want smooth action without drawing every frame, learn **Synfig Studio** or try the simpler freeware **Pivot Animator**. Avoid Blender and OpenToonz until you have a solid grasp of animation basics, as they will likely frustrate a beginner.\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":129,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-31T19:08:14Z","content":"The research is complete. Here are the definitive answers for both of your questions:\n\n### 1. Is Motion Canvas 100% Free?\n**Yes. It is 100% free forever.** \nMotion Canvas is released under the MIT License (the most permissive open-source license). \n*   **No paid tiers:** Unlike tools like Remotion which require a commercial license for large companies, Motion Canvas has no premium version.\n*   **Full Monetization:** You own 100% of the rights to the videos you make. You can upload them to YouTube, monetize them, or sell them for client work without paying a single cent to the creators.\n\n***\n\n### 2. Best Tools for \"Stickman\" or \"Sketching\" Animation\nMotion Canvas is terrible for drawing stickmen. If you want a hand-drawn or whiteboard style for YouTube, here are the best open-source/free tools ranked by how easy they are for beginners:\n\n#### Option A: The \"Whiteboard / Hand-Drawn Sketch\" Style\n**Winner: Excalidraw** (Easiest)\n*   **Why:** Excalidraw is a free web whiteboard where *everything* you draw automatically gets a rough, hand-sketched look. If you want to make videos that look like you are drawing concepts on a whiteboard in real-time, you just use Excalidraw, record your screen, and talk. \n*   **Runner up:** **Krita**. If you actually want to draw frame-by-frame (like flipping a notepad), Krita is a free digital painting app that has an amazing, built-in animation timeline.\n\n#### Option B: The \"Action Stickman\" Style (Rigging / Bones)\nIf you don't want to draw 30 frames just to make a stickman wave his hand, you need a software that uses \"Bones\" (rigging). You draw the stickman once, put a skeleton in him, and drag his hand. The software mathematically fills in the frames.\n*   **Winner: Synfig Studio** (Moderate difficulty). It is open-source and specializes in this kind of 2D vector movement.\n*   **The Classic Beginner Choice: Pivot Animator.** (Freeware). If you just want to make classic stick-figure fight animations, Pivot is literally designed to do exactly that with zero learning curve.\n\n#### What to Avoid:\n*   **Blender (Grease Pencil)** & **OpenToonz**: These are incredibly powerful open-source tools (OpenToonz is used by Studio Ghibli), but they are **massive overkill** for a beginner. The interface will give you a headache if you just want to animate a stickman. \n\n**My Advice:** If you are trying to make a YouTube explainer video that feels like you are sketching concepts on paper, open **Excalidraw** right now in your browser, draw some shapes, and see how much you like the sketchy aesthetic!","thinking":"**Synthesizing Findings**\n\nI've synthesized the findings and determined that Subagent 2 is complete. I've also confirmed that no tools were necessary for this stage. Now, I'm working to consolidate this information into a concise, actionable output for the user.\n\n\n"}
